# Writing Your Own Effects { .heading-no-number }

In the previous chapter we saw that Koka tracks effects in types and that
handlers implement effects. Now we'll write our own effects from scratch —
and discover that this lets us define entirely new control-flow abstractions
as ordinary library code.

---

## Defining an Effect

Use the `effect` keyword to declare a new effect and its _operations_:

```koka
effect emit
  fun emit( msg : string ) : ()
```

This declares an effect `:emit` with one operation, also called `emit`. Any function
that calls `emit(...)` must carry the `:emit` effect in its type:

```koka
fun log-example0() : emit ()
  emit("starting...")
  emit("step 1")
  emit("done!")
```

Notice `log-example0` doesn't decide _how_ emitted messages are handled.
That decision is deferred to whoever _calls_ `log-example0`.

---

## Writing a Handler

A handler provides the implementation for each operation. Use `with handler`:

```koka
fun log-example() : emit ()
  emit("starting...")
  emit("done!")

fun example1()
  with handler
    fun emit(msg) println("[LOG] " ++ msg)
  log-example()
```

Running this prints:
```unchecked
[LOG] starting...
[LOG] done!
```

The handler intercepts each `emit` call and runs the provided body.

---

## Swapping Implementations

The same `log-example` function works with any handler. Let's collect all messages
into a list instead of printing them:

```koka
fun collect-messages( action : () -> <emit|e> () ) : e list<string>
  var messages := []
  with handler
    return(x)     messages.reverse
    fun emit(msg) messages := Cons(msg, messages)
  action()

fun example2()
  val msgs = collect-messages(log-example)
  println(msgs.show)   // ["starting...", "done!"]
```

The `return` clause in the handler transforms the final value — when `action()` finishes
normally, the handler returns `messages.reverse` instead of `()`.

---

## Effect Abstraction

You can store a handler in a `val` and reuse it:

```koka
val silent = handler
  fun emit(_) ()   // discard all messages

fun example6()
  with silent
  log-example()   // prints nothing
```

This is the same as any other first-class function value — it just happens to
implement an effect.

---

## Value Operations: Dynamic Binding

A common pattern is a value that flows through all code in scope without
being threaded as a parameter. For example, a pretty printer that needs
a display width:

```koka
effect val width : int

fun pretty-line( line : string ) : width string
  line.truncate(width)

fun pretty-example()
  with val width = 40
  println(pretty-line("hello, a long world of documentation strings!"))
```

The `effect val` declaration creates an operation that behaves like a
dynamically scoped variable. Any function in scope can read `width`
without it being passed explicitly, and the type system tracks the
`:width` effect. Under the hood, `with val width = 40` desugars to
`with fun width() 40` -- it is just a tail-resumptive handler that
always returns the same value.

---

## A State Handler

Let's build a state effect from scratch. We need two operations,
`get` and `set`:

```koka
effect state<a>
  fun get() : a
  fun set( x : a ) : ()
```

A function that uses state:

```koka
fun sumdown( sum : int = 0 ) : <state<int>,div> int
  val i = get()
  if i <= 0 then sum
  else
    set(i - 1)
    sumdown(sum + i)
```

The handler uses a local `var` to hold the state:

```koka
fun state( init : a, action : () -> <state<a>,div|e> b ) : <div|e> b
  var st := init
  with handler
    fun get()  st
    fun set(i) st := i
  action()

fun state-example()
  println(state(10){ sumdown() }.show)   // 55
```

We can also return the final state alongside the result by adding a
`return` clause:

```koka
fun pstate( init : a, action : () -> <state<a>,div|e> b ) : <div|e> (b, a)
  var st := init
  with handler
    return(x)  (x, st)
    fun get()  st
    fun set(i) st := i
  action()

fun pstate-example()
  println(pstate(10){ sumdown() }.show)   // (55, 0)
```

The `return` clause transforms the final value -- when the action completes,
the handler pairs it with the final state.

---

## Composing Handlers

Effect handlers compose freely. This is one of their fundamental advantages
over monads (which need transformers) or `call/cc` (which is not delimited).

Suppose we have a function that raises an error on odd state:

```koka
fun no-odds() : <raise,state<int>> int
  val i = get()
  if i.is-odd then raise("no odds")
  else
    set(i / 2)
    i
```

We can handle both effects by composing handlers:

```koka
fun state-raise(init) : div (maybe<int>, int)
  with pstate(init)
  with catch-as-maybe
  no-odds()
```

The order matters: `pstate` is the outer handler, so the result type is
`(maybe<int>, int)` -- a pair of the maybe-result and the final state.
The state reflects whatever happened up to the point `raise` was called.

---

## Effects That Resume

Some effects need to _resume_ execution at the call site — they're not just interruptions.
Use `ctl` (control) instead of `fun` for these:

```koka
effect ask
  ctl ask() : int

fun ask-twice() : ask int
  ask() + ask()

fun example3()
  with ctl ask() resume(21)
  println(ask-twice().show)   // 42
```

When `ask()` is called, the handler intercepts it and calls `resume(21)` — which
continues execution at the call site with the value `21`. Since `ask-twice` calls
`ask()` twice, `resume` runs twice, and the result is `42`.

Think of it this way: `fun` operations work like function calls (always return),
while `ctl` operations work like coroutines that can be resumed with a value.

You can also resume with effectful values supplied by another effect:

```unchecked
import std/num/random
```
```koka
fun ask-random() : random int
  with ctl ask() resume(random-int())
  ask-twice()
```

---

## A Real Example: Exceptions

Let's build our own exception effect from scratch:

```koka
effect raise
  final ctl raise( msg : string ) : a

fun safe-divide( x : int, y : int ) : raise int
  if y == 0 then raise("division by zero")
  else x / y

// Handler 1: print and return default
fun catch-with-default( action : () -> <raise,console|e> int ) : <console|e> int
  with final ctl raise(msg)
    println("caught: " ++ msg)
    -1
  action()

// Handler 2: wrap result in maybe
fun catch-as-maybe( action : () -> <raise|e> a ) : e maybe<a>
  with handler
    return(x)           Just(x)
    final ctl raise(_)  Nothing
  action()

fun example4()
  println(show(catch-with-default{ safe-divide(10, 0) }))    // caught: division by zero \n -1
  println(show(catch-as-maybe{ safe-divide(10, 2) }))         // Just(5)
  println(show(catch-as-maybe{ safe-divide(10, 0) }))         // Nothing
```

`final ctl` marks an operation that never resumes — the handler takes over entirely.
This is more efficient for exceptions (no need to save a resumption).

---

## A Real Example: Backtracking

Effects can resume _more than once_ to explore multiple possibilities:

```koka
effect ctl choice() : bool

fun xor() : choice bool
  val p = choice()
  val q = choice()
  if p then !q else q

fun all-choices( action : () -> <choice|e> a ) : e list<a>
  with handler
    return(x)    [x]
    ctl choice() resume(False) ++ resume(True)
  action()

fun example5()
  println(all-choices(xor).show)   // [False, True, True, False]
```

The handler calls `resume` twice — once with `False` and once with `True` — collecting
all possible results. This is backtracking search, implemented as a library!

---

## Summary

In this section you learned:
- Defining effects with `effect` and `ctl`/`fun`/`val` operations
- Writing handlers with `with handler`
- The `return` clause to transform the final value
- `fun` for tail-resumptive operations (always resume once)
- `val` for dynamically scoped values
- `ctl` for general control operations (can resume any number of times)
- `final ctl` for non-resuming operations like exceptions
- Building a state handler from scratch with `get`/`set`
- Composing handlers freely, where ordering determines semantics
- Effects as a unifying mechanism for exceptions, state, backtracking, and more

